        <technology>
            <technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>In this model, there are similarities to its global counterpart. This includes batch preparation, melting and refining, forming, and post-forming. In this model, it is assumed that all the processes are contained in this one process. During batch preparation, the pretreated raw materials are mixed together. Then, once in the melting process, they will be mixed together in elevated temperatures. This is followed up by the refining process, where the molten glass is homogenized and conditioned after it is freed of bubbles that could harm its formation. After refining, the molten glass flows to bushings, where it is drawn through openings in the bushings by a mechanical force to be fiberized. Immediately after leaving the bushings, the fibers are subjected to rapid cooling by air and water spray. A coating also referred to as size, is applied to the fibers before they enter the subsequent post-forming processes to protect and lubricate the brittle filaments. The size applied also dictates the performance of the fibers in a composite, notably the interface interaction between the matrix and the filaments. The size is generally an aqueous mixture of polymers, lubricants, coupling agents, and additives. The exact composition, however, depends on the glass fiber being manufactured and remains proprietary. After sizing, the filaments are wound into a spun.

The activity starts when the material enter the process. The materials entering the process are water, chemicals, energy uses and infrastructure. In this model, the infrastructure covered is the furnace and the sizing machine. Others were not included due to data limitations. There is no direct emission to soil, but there are the direct emission to air such as NO2, SO2, PM, HF, HCl, and CO2 comes from melting &amp; refining process also water vapour comes from spraying in forming process and drying in post-forming process. The sizing materials (e.g. epoxy resins, boric oxide, dimethyldichlorosilane, and ethylene glycol) consumption is accounted as well in this model. The sizing materials remain the property of the glass fiber. The sizing materials that do not coat the final product generated along with the waste water. The hazardous waste comes from waste lube oil. The consumption of lubricating oil using a thermalbonding machine as a proxy. 

PBL Netherlands Environmental Assessment Agency, 2019. Decarbonisation options for the Dutch glass fibre industry. PBL-ECN Part of TNO.
Dai, Q., Kelly, J., Sullivan, J. and Elgowainy, A., 2015. Life-Cycle Analysis Update of Glass and Glass Fiber for the GREETTM Model.</technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>
            <technologicalApplicability>na</technologicalApplicability>
